## 2. The Ear Learns the Landscape

Language enters development through the senses before it enters through the mouth.

The auditory system begins processing speech-related information well before the child can reproduce speech. Newborns show sensitivity to features of speech rhythm and prosody, including aspects of the language environment encountered during gestation. This creates continuity across the transition from womb to world: some of the earliest sounds that help organize attention after birth are sounds the infant has already encountered before birth. 

But the newborn's auditory system is not yet committed to one language.

Early in life, infants can discriminate a broad range of phonetic distinctions. Over the first year, that broad sensitivity becomes increasingly tuned to the structure of experienced languages. Native-language contrasts become easier to discriminate while sensitivity to some less-experienced contrasts declines.

This process is often called **perceptual narrowing**.

The phrase can be misleading if it suggests simple deterioration. What is happening is better understood as specialization. The infant brain is becoming more efficient at processing the patterns that matter in its environment. 

A child growing up with one language is gradually tuned toward the regularities of that language. A child exposed to several languages is adapting to multiple systems. The developing brain is not being handed a universal phonetic dictionary and then deleting most of it. It is becoming increasingly responsive to the statistical structure of the linguistic world the child actually inhabits.

That distinction is important because it reveals one of the general principles of development established in earlier chapters.

Biology provides possibilities.

Experience calibrates them.

The calibration is neither arbitrary nor completely predetermined. The infant arrives with powerful perceptual capacities, but those capacities become reorganized through exposure and use.

The same principle operates in speech segmentation.

To an adult listener, fluent speech appears to arrive already divided into words. To an infant, however, speech is initially a continuous acoustic stream. The child has to determine where one unit ends and another begins.

One way the developing system accomplishes this is by tracking statistical regularities.

If one syllable reliably predicts another, their transitional probability is relatively high. If the likelihood drops sharply after a particular syllable, that drop provides information about a possible word boundary. In classic laboratory experiments, infants as young as eight months have demonstrated the ability to extract such regularities from continuous streams of artificial speech. 

No one has to point at the boundaries and explain them.

The infant is detecting structure.

This is a direct connection to Chapter 5's account of the developing mind as an active learner and Chapter 6's account of exploration and prediction. The child is not passively receiving a finished linguistic system. The child is selecting information, detecting regularities and gradually constructing representations from the evidence available.

But statistical learning alone is not enough.

A child can detect that certain sounds regularly occur together without knowing what those sounds mean.

Meaning requires another step.

