## 4. The First Symbol May Be a Point

Before children speak in sentences, they can communicate about things that are not themselves.

A child points toward a dog.

Looks at the dog.

Looks back toward the parent.

The adult follows the child's attention.

The child has coordinated two minds around one thing.

Pointing is deceptively simple. Developmentally, it is extraordinary.

The infant is no longer limited to acting directly on an object. The infant can alter another person's attention from a distance. The gesture becomes a tool for directing another mind.

This is why gesture deserves a more important place in the story of language than the phrase "pre-language" might suggest. Pointing, showing, reaching and other gestures can express communicative intention before the child has sufficient spoken language to express the same relational content. Gesture also frequently precedes or accompanies early speech and has been associated longitudinally with later vocabulary and grammar. 

A pointing child is not merely indicating a location.

The child is creating a shared reference.

That is already an important property of symbols.

The object exists in the world.

The point does not.

Its significance comes from the relationship between people, gesture and referent.

This also helps explain why gesture and speech can temporarily complement one another. A child may point toward an apple and say something resembling "eat." The gesture specifies the object while the sound indicates the action. The child can therefore communicate a relationship before being able to express the entire relationship verbally.

The important qualification is that naturally emerging gesture should not be confused with commercial programs claiming that teaching arbitrary signs will reliably accelerate language development. The evidence for such programs in typically developing hearing infants is inconclusive. Gesture is clearly part of communication; that does not mean every gesture-training intervention produces additional language growth. 

Again, prediction and causation are different.

A child who gestures early may develop language earlier partly because gesture reflects a broader underlying capacity for attention, communication, motor control and social engagement.

The gesture may be a scaffold.

It may also be a marker.

The evidence does not require choosing between those possibilities.

