## 5. The Mouth Learns by Trying

Babbling looks like noise only if one ignores what happens before and after it.

Beginning around the latter part of the first year, infants produce increasingly rhythmic consonant-vowel sequences: *ba-ba*, *da-da*, *ma-ma*. These sounds are not yet words, but they represent increasingly controlled exploration of the vocal system.

The infant produces a sound.

Hears the result.

Produces another.

Changes the movement.

Repeats a successful pattern.

The process resembles the exploratory learning described in Chapter 6. Action generates information, and information modifies subsequent action.

The vocal system becomes an experimental instrument.

Auditory feedback tells the infant what was produced. Motor feedback tells the infant how it was produced. Social feedback tells the infant what other people do with it.

A caregiver may imitate the sound.

The child may repeat it.

The caregiver responds again.

A turn-taking rhythm emerges.

This is one reason babbling belongs in the history of communication rather than being treated merely as a precursor to speech.

It is simultaneously motor practice, sensory learning and social participation.

The social environment can influence how infants vocalize, although the evidence is stronger for immediate interactional shaping than for a single causal pathway from caregiver response to later language. Babbling should therefore not be interpreted as a simple performance test of intelligence, nor should differences in vocal milestones automatically be translated into judgments about parenting. 

And the developmental principle extends beyond spoken language.

Research on infants exposed to sign language has identified rhythmic, repetitive manual behaviors sometimes described as manual babbling. That observation is theoretically important because it separates the developmental capacity for language production from the mere maturation of the vocal tract.

The infant is learning a language system.

The system need not be spoken to be real.

