# 15. From Words to Culture

The deepest transformation produced by language cannot be measured simply by counting words.

A child who learns a word for "fire" has acquired more than a sound-object association.

The child has acquired a piece of a symbolic system shared by other minds.

That system can describe things that are not present.

It can refer to the past.

It can anticipate the future.

It can describe another person's feelings.

It can transmit instructions.

It can tell stories about people who died long before the child was born.

It can preserve knowledge that the child could never discover independently.

This is why language is more than communication.

It is a mechanism of cultural transmission.

Children learn not only from physical objects and events but from people who communicate intentions, categories, practices and socially shared meanings. Cultural-learning theories emphasize the development of intention-reading, pattern detection and participation in shared practices, although the complete theoretical pathway remains a matter of interpretation rather than a single experimentally demonstrated mechanism. 

The scientific claim can nevertheless be stated with confidence:

language supports communication, categorization, memory, instruction, coordination and the transfer of knowledge.

Its cultural significance is therefore enormous.

A child learns the name of an object.

Then learns what people do with it.

Then learns what it means.

Then learns what people say about it.

Then learns what people believe about it.

Eventually, language allows the child to participate in conversations about things that are entirely absent from immediate experience.

This is the point at which development becomes cumulative.

The child inherits knowledge that no individual infant could reconstruct alone.

