# The Child Enters a Shared World

The first word, then, is not the beginning.

It is a threshold.

Before it comes the cry that recruits another person.

The gaze that becomes reciprocal.

The vocal turn that invites another turn.

The ear that detects the statistical structure of speech.

The pointing finger that directs another mind.

The babbling mouth that experiments with sound.

The memory that preserves a tentative association.

The shared attention that makes reference possible.

The repeated encounter that turns a tentative mapping into a more durable word.

Then words begin to combine.

Patterns become grammar.

Conversation becomes increasingly flexible.

Language becomes a medium for remembering, imagining, explaining, asking, persuading and teaching.

And something fundamental has changed.

The child is no longer merely learning about the world.

Through shared attention, vocal exchange, gesture and emerging symbols, the child is beginning to participate in a symbolic world already inhabited by other people. 

This is why language belongs where it does in the developmental sequence of this book.

Chapter 4 described relationship as a regulatory system.

Chapter 5 described the emergence of the active mind.

Chapter 6 described learning through action, prediction, exploration and memory.

Chapter 7 describes what happens when learning becomes shared meaning.

The infant discovers that other people do not merely regulate the body or provide information about the physical world.

They inhabit a world of symbols.

And those symbols can be shared.

That may be the most extraordinary inheritance a child receives.

Before the child can speak, other people have already built a world of meaning.

Language is the invitation into it.

And once the invitation is accepted, the child is no longer learning only from what happens.

The child can begin learning from what someone else knows. 
